<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine II of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 952</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Causantín mac Áeda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine II was king of Alba, the kingdom that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, from about 900 to 943, one of the longest reigns of early Scottish kings. He submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamont-bridge">Eamont Bridge</a> in 927 but joined the coalition defeated by Æthelstan at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunanburh">Battle of Brunanburh</a> in 937. He abdicated to become a monk at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> and died in 952.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantine was a grandson of Kenneth I, the first of the family recorded as a king, though as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>. His father Áed succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> in 876 and was killed in 878, so Constantine cannot have been born later than 879. He may have spent a period in exile in Ireland, where his aunt Máel Muire was married to two successive High Kings. He became king on the death of his cousin Donald II, who was probably killed fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> at Dunnottar in 900. The change of title from king of the Picts to king of Alba belongs to a wider transformation of Pictland, and the origins of the Kingdom of Alba are traced to his lifetime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 906 Constantine and Bishop Cellach met at the Hill of Belief near Scone and pledged that the laws and disciplines of the faith should be kept. Historians debate the meaning of the phrase pariter cum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scoti">Scottis</a>. Around 918 he and Ealdred son of Eadulf fought Ragnall at Corbridge, a battle that appears to have been indecisive. In 920 or 921 he was among the kings who chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> as father and lord, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> then first uses the word scottas for his subjects.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamont-bridge">Eamont Bridge</a> on 12 July 927 Constantine, Owain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a> and Ealdred agreed to renounce all idolatry, that is, not to ally with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> kings. In 934 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> invaded by land and sea and ravaged southern Alba, with no record of battles. Constantine witnessed a charter with him as subregulus, but there is no record that he submitted to Æthelstan's overlordship. In 937 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-guthfrithson">Olaf Guthfrithson</a> and Owain ap Dyfnwal against Æthelstan and was defeated at Brunanburh, where a son named Cellach was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Constantine abdicated in 943 and entered a monastery, leaving the kingdom to his nephew Malcolm I. The Prophecy of Berchán states that this was not voluntary. The monastery was probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, refounded in his reign and given to the Céli Dé reformers, and he is said to have been abbot there. He died in 952. His reign of 43 years is believed to have played a defining part in the Gaelicisation of Pictland, and the words Scots and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> were first used in his time. The form of kingship that appeared then lasted until the Davidian Revolution of the 12th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine II of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edward’s son Æthelstan completed the work: in 927 he took York from its Viking rulers and on 12 July received the submission of the northern rulers, including <b>Constantine II of Scotland</b>, at Eamont Bridge, an event usually taken as the founding moment of a single English kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 900, under Donald II and <b>Constantine II</b>, the kingdom was called Alba, with its royal centre at Scone and relics of Columba at Dunkeld.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57,-5&amp;year=942" aria-label="Scotland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4442 230 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantine II of Scotland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4892 500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4892 500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57,-5&amp;year=942">What was here in 942 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
